## Course overview

Economics at Standard Level introduces the central question of how scarce resources are allocated. Students learn to think like economists, building models, weighing trade-offs, and evaluating real-world policy using the nine **key concepts** of the course: scarcity, choice, efficiency, equity, economic well-being, sustainability, change, interdependence, and intervention.

## What you will study

- **Introduction**: scarcity, choice, opportunity cost, and the role of markets.
- **Microeconomics**: demand and supply, elasticity, market failure, and government intervention.
- **Macroeconomics**: economic activity, inflation, unemployment, and fiscal and monetary policy.
- **The global economy**: international trade, exchange rates, economic integration, and development.

## Assessment

| Component | Weighting | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Paper 1 | 30% | Extended-response questions |
| Paper 2 | 40% | Data-response questions |
| Internal Assessment | 30% | Portfolio of three commentaries |

## The Internal Assessment

The IA is a **portfolio of three commentaries**, each analysing a recent news article through the lens of a different part of the syllabus. You apply economic theory and diagrams to real events such as a tariff, a subsidy, or a central-bank decision, then evaluate the outcomes.
